{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 Garmin workout HR range export zone target change' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n...\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n...\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n...\n\nWould it be possible to either:\n\n1. Tag HR values explicitly as absolute bpm in the Garmin Connect sync payload, restoring pre-30.18 behavior, or\n2. Translate HR-bpm targets into power-equivalent zones at sync time (if a power meter is available on the athlete’s profile).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin planned workout sync — multi-target steps & zone-only targets - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-planned-workout-sync-multi-target-steps-zone-only-targets/130551\nPublished: 2026-06-29T17:42:31.000Z\nAuthor: Il_Jae_Lee\nHighlights:\nTwo related enhancements to how planned workouts sync to Garmin that would make structured sessions translate more faithfully on the watch:\n\n1. Multi-target steps (e.g. HR and Power)\n\nCurrently a workout step syncs with a single target type. It would be great to attach more than one target to the same step — for example a Power primary target with a secondary HR cap/ceiling, or a Power target plus an HR guardrail. This is especially useful for easy/long runs where I want to govern by HR but keep a power ceiling, and for quality work where I want power as the lead with HR as a sanity check. Garmin’s own workout format supports a target plus a secondary/“do not exceed” constraint, so this would mostly be a matter of passing the second target through on sync.\n\n2. Zone-only targets instead of explicit ranges\n\nWhen a step is built against a zone (e.g. “Z2”), it would be ideal to sync it to Garmin as that zone reference rather than expanding it to a fixed min/max range. Two benefits: (a) the watch displays the zone the athlete actually trains by, and (b) if zone boundaries change (FTP/LTHR update), the workout stays correct without rebuilding it. Right now a hardcoded range can drift out of sync with current zones.\n\nBoth would meaningfully improve fidelity between the Intervals.icu calendar and what shows up on the Garmin head unit. Thanks for the great work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing Pace & HR targets to Garmin - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-pace-hr-targets-to-garmin/130238\nPublished: 2026-06-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nNew to intervals.icu and trying to understand the workout syntax a bit better. I’m using my own dev tool to push my plan to intervals.icu with the API, that then syncs it to my Garmin watch. But having a bit of trouble getting the pace targets to sync. Garmin shows ‘No targets’ for my repeats. Currently, this is how my example run looks like\n\n```auto\n- 22m Z2 HR\n\n4x\n- Strides 20s Z4-Z5 Pace\n- Walk 60s\n\n```\n\nInterestingly, removing the first 22mins and just having the whole workout as 4x repeats somehow seems to be working. Any help with this would be appreciated 🙂\n\n...\n\nEdit: Is it with combining multiple targets?\n\nI changed the ‘Auto’ to ‘Pace’ and the pace synced well for the strides, but HR for easy run says ‘No Target’ now.\n\n...\n\nIntervals hasn’t figured out yet how to send workouts over the Garmin API with multiple targets.\nLet’s be clear: Intervals is NOT sending FIT workouts to Garmin because Garmin doesn’t allow that. Intervals sends a workout in a script format to the Garmin API and Garmin Connect translates that to a workout on your device. The target type definition is not clear or Garmin is somehow blocking that.\nThis is kind of a hot topic over the last couple of months but the solution hasn’t been found yet.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## What happens\n\nOn a workout synced to a Suunto 9 Peak, a step whose target is pace shows the step\nname, `Avg`, `Target`, the value and the gauge. A step whose target is heart rate\nshows the step name and the ordinary running fields only – no target, no range, no\ngauge, nothing that says what to hold.\n\nI could not tell from the watch whether this was my syntax, so I built one workout with\neleven steps, one target form each, and walked through all of them:\n\n| step | written as | on the watch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 zone | `90s Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 2 pctmax one | `90s 85% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 3 pctmax rng | `90s 82-87% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 4 thr one | `90s 90% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 5 thr rng | `90s 88-94% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 6 zone span | `90s Z2-Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 7 zone p | `90s Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 8 pct p one | `90s 40% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 9 pct p rng | `90s 60-70% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 10 abs p | `90s 7:00 pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 11 dist | `400mtr Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n\nAll six heart-rate forms behave identically. All five pace forms work.\n\n## Why I think the data is not the problem\n\nIntervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way\nin the workout view, and they are in the workout export\n(`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.json`) as a `_hr` object:\n\n```auto\n1 zone \"_hr\": {\"start\": 153.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n2 pctmax one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 159.0, \"start\": 156.0, \"end\": 162.0}\n3 pctmax rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 158.5, \"start\": 154.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n4 thr one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 154.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 157.0}\n5 thr rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 156.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 161.0}\n6 zone span \"_hr\": {\"start\": 146.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n\n```\n\nAnd the SuuntoPlus Guide format has a field for exactly this shape. From Suunto’s own\nGuide Cloud API document, the sample guide’s warm-up step:\n\n```json\n\"fields\": [\n  { \"type\": \"targetHeartRate\", \"value\": 111, \"min\": 104, \"max\": 148 },\n  { \"type\": \"heartRate\" },\n  ...\n]\n\n```\n\n`targetHeartRate` takes absolute bpm as a value with optional min/max, and Suunto’s own\nexample uses it on a heart-rate step.\n\n## And the watch can display it\n\nA structured workout built in the Suunto app on the same watch, with heart-rate bands\n(warm-up 130-140, interval 150-160, recovery 100-110), shows the target and the gauge\nnormally during the heart-rate steps. So the watch and its target display are not the\nlimitation.\n\n...\n\nthank you! I built a workout with ONLY heart-rate steps and all three show\nthe target and the dial, perfectly.\n\n...\n\nYou could try to set it in Intervals as ‘Auto’.\nI don’t know if a mix will then work in Suunto, but there was recently some work done to get this supported on Garmin.\n\nIt’s strange that we don’t see those selectors on your screenshot. If you select HR, the Intervals graph should show the workout coloured in HR zones. If Pace is selected, it would be in Pace zone colors. Auto will show it depending on your priority settings.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target missing from Garmin Connect workout upload\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-missing-from-garmin-connect-workout-upload/5483\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n- the workout “steps-skeleton” is correct:\n\n...\n\n- but the HR intensity targets are completely missing.\n\n...\n\nLook for the “hr=1s” on steps. It doesn’t look like your workouts have that.\n\n...\n\nIt’s not a bug and it’s not a workaround. The screen tells you that you have to choose Instant HR for Garmin watch.\nBut there are people using something else then Garmin watch… The other settings are for head units, Edge for example.\nI’m not sure, but possibly it is enough to make the choice ones to make it default on your account.\n\n...\n\nGarmin Connect knows what device you have.\nYou don’t have to “type” ‘hr=1s’. You can but you can also choose the Instant HR option in the workout builder.\nDid you search the forum for both terms “Instant HR” and “Lap HR”?\nThat might explain quite a bit.\n\n...\n\nHmm. Maybe I should change the default. I initially went with Lap HR because that makes sense for cycling and thats where Intervals.icu started. I think instant HR would also be ok for cycling. Your HR doesn’t change all that quickly.\n\n...\n\nPersonally, I have never asked myself the problem. I’ve always gone with instant HR, it seemed to me to be the best solution and it always satisfied me. Rather, I would find it more useful to add a range of Hr min and Hr max to keep, as for watts. For some athletes I take the test for DFA a1 and determined the aerobic threshold that cannot be determined in % on HR is so personal that I would like to set the target manually. For the rest, I think what’s there is great.\n\n...\n\nI searched forums, but I can’t really find useful info about Lap-HR: if there is any benefit to use “Lap-HR” for target. @MedTechCD: I’ve used TrainingPeaks workout builder quite a lot and I’ve never seen any refinments of TargetHR. I simply chose either a zone (i.e., an interval) or a specific target (i.e., specific value), and “it just works”. Always. No selection if “lap” vs. “instant”. There is neither an option to choose if the intended device is a bike computer or a hand watch.\nTherefore it must be possible to “make right” for external API users w/o bothering with the device type. If TP can do what ever is needed to end up in the correct encoding in the FIT, then others could do it too.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Structured running workouts from intervals to Garmin Connect - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/structured-running-workouts-from-intervals-to-garmin-connect/130705\nPublished: 2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nDid you setup your Pace zones?\n\nAccording to that thread, mixing HR and Pace is work in progress.\n\n> @Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions:\n> \n> You are correct, we fixed it (for Garmin) now but the feature is not complete so I was waiting to make the announcement slight_smile There are some tweaks still to be made\n\n...\n\nThey will not be rendered on Garmin Connect in the way a Garmin Connect workout is rendered. In Connect, you will see them as formatted text, not-editable in Connect (that’s intentional by Garmin because they don’t want workouts from other sources to be editable in Connect). But that formatted text will result in a correct workout when synced to your device (if the syntax is correct).\n\nThe mix of targets (HR/Power/Pace) wasn’t working with workouts from Intervals until now, but is being addressed now as you can see in the linked posts by @R2Tom.\nOnly one target type per workout was working correctly. Let’s see if @eva can have the feature fully supported in the next days.\n\n...\n\n5m 08:00 (0.62km) Pace\n5m 08:00-10:00 (0.56km) Pace\n5m Z2 HR (142-150bpm)\n\n...\n\nNot sure on how to pass it through the API, but you should not pass the distance like that. In the workout builder, it clearly shows that passing the distance before the target type, confuses the algo. Either put the distance after the target type or don’t pass it at all.\nAnd you don’t need to pass the absolute HR in between brackets either because Intervals adds that automatically deduced from the Z2 HR.\n\nTry passing this: (works in the builder)\n-5m 08:00 Pace (0.62km)\n-5m 08:00-10:00 Pace (0.56km)\n-5m Z2 HR\n-2km 07:00 Pace\n\n...\n\n> @R2Tom:\n> \n> Did you setup your Pace zones?\n\n@Wim you should setup your pace zones on the settings page for activity type running\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @Razvan_Zlavog\nPost date: 17 July 2026 20:08 UTC\n\n+1 to R2Tom — setting a Run threshold pace was the fix. With threshold_pace unset, intervals drops the per-step pace targets on the Garmin export (falls back to HR), hence “No Target”. After setting it, the pace targets showed up on the watch (confirmed on a Forerunner). One catch: set the threshold first, then push a fresh event — existing ones need a delete + recreate to re-trigger the Garmin export. Full write-up: Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc)\n\n...\n\nHere is an example workout text format for you to take a look at. The workout is entered in Markdown.\n\n...\n\n## Day 3: Build Speed\n\n## WU: 15’ in HRZ 1-3\n\n-15m 68-80.5% HR\n\n4x\n\n- 30s 90% HR\n- 1m 68% HR\n\n## MS: 6-8 x 2’ at SPZ 4 w/ 2’ RI\n\n8x\n\n- 2m 80-85% HR\n- 2m 68% HR\n\n## CD: 5’ in HRZ 1\n\n- 5m 67.5-72% HR\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing both pace and heart rate to Garmin Connect workout\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-both-pace-and-heart-rate-to-garmin-connect-workout/67330\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nJust to be clear, if I choose “HR” when importing the .fit workout in Intervals, my Garmin watch will not display the correct pace for my 400 meter intervals. It just says, “400m” for the run portion instead of 400m: 4:25 min/km - 4:40 min/km.\n\nHowever, if I directly add the .fit file to my Garmin watch (by connecting it via USB to my computer), I do have both pace and HR data for each workout step, so I think the .fit file is correctly formatted.\n\n...\n\nI encountered this issue as well. If a workout only consists of HR or Pace targets then the output from intervals.icu syncs as expected - any combination thereafter and only a single target type is generated.\n\nFrom intervals.icu I exported a fit file and using (FIT file viewer – RUNALYZE) I could see the HR targets were correct but pace was left as “open”.\n\nI then tried to reproduce a fit file on intervals.icu that was originally created on Garmin Connect:\n\n1. create simple workout with HR target steps and Pace target steps\n2. sync to watch\n3. connect watch to computer and grab the *_workout.fit file\n4. import the fit file to intervals.icu (the plain text steps appear as expected)\n5. export and sync the fit file generated by intervals.icu\n\nIn (5) above the fit file produced does not contain the correct target type information (File Types | FIT SDK | Garmin Developers). Details of the simple workout are listed below.\n\nSo, I believe there is a bug in the code that is generating fit files given:\n\n1. use of “open” target type\n2. inability to reproduce a simple uploaded fit file originally generated by Garmin Connect\n\nUnfortunately, it seems the only potential workaround is to externally generate a correct fit file using Python (or other utility/language - though there are quite a few Python options on GitHub).\n\n## Note:\n\nThe default workout order on the settings page (“Power, HR, Pace”) does not correctly handle this. Changing it to (“Pace, HR, Power”) will result in pace but then HR targets are “open” in the generated fit file. Ideally, the plain text would prefer the first target type listed so one could properly create mixed target type workouts.\n\n## Reference:\n\nWorkout plain text:\n\n```md\n- Warmup 5m Z1 HR\n- 15m Z2 HR\n- 5m 89-94% Pace\n\n```\n\n...\n\n```auto\nSUCCESS\n# File size: 1197, protocol version: 1.00, profile_version: 21.96\n# File header CRC: expected=0x014F, calculated=0x014F\n= TYPE=0 NAME=file_id NUMBER=0\n--- serial_number=1318830246=1318830246\n--- time_created=1125862911=2025-09-03T19:41:51Z\n--- manufacturer=1=garmin\n--- garmin_product=65534=connect\n--- type=5=workout\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=file_creator NUMBER=49\n--- software_version=2517=2517\n--- hardware_version=0=0\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=workout NUMBER=26\n--- capabilities=32=protected=0,resistance=0,grade=0,power=0,cadence=0,distance=0,heart_rate=0,speed=0,tcx=32,new_leaf=0,firstbeat=0,fitness_equipment=0,custom=0,interval=0\n--- wkt_name=\"FF1_TEST\"\n--- xxx10=417577=417577\n--- num_valid_steps=3=3\n--- sport=1=running\n--- xxx9=1=1\n--- sub_sport=0=generic\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=workout_step NUMBER=27\n--- duration_time=300000=300.000 s\n--- target_hr_zone=1=1\n--- message_index=0=selected=0,reserved=0,mask=0\n--- duration_type=0=time\n--- target_type=1=heart_rate\n--- intensity=2=warmup\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=workout_step NUMBER=27\n--- duration_time=900000=900.000 s\n--- target_hr_zone=2=2\n--- message_index=1=selected=0,reserved=0,mask=1\n--- duration_type=0=time\n--- target_type=1=heart_rate\n--- intensity=0=active\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=workout_step NUMBER=27\n--- duration_time=300000=300.000 s\n--- target_speed_zone=0=0\n--- custom_target_speed_low=2823=10.163 km/h\n--- custom_target_speed_high=2980=10.728 km/h\n--- message_index=2=selected=0,reserved=0,mask=2\n--- duration_type=0=time\n--- target_type=0=speed\n--- intensity=0=active\n==\n# CRC: expected=0xCD32, calculated=0xCD32\n\n...\n\nI’ve also seen this on a Garmin 970 for this workout.\n\nWarmup\n\n- Easy w/up 10m Z1 HR\n\nMain\n\n- Relaxed 26m Z2 HR\n\nStrides 4x\n\n- Stride 20s 04:00/km Pace\n- Easy 1m Z1 HR\n\nRecovery\n\n- Cooldown 3m Z1HR\n\nWhat I’ve found is that if I select “Pace” then the pace intervals come through perfectly, but if I set HR, then the HR intervals come through perfectly.\n\nI can create the workout directly in Garmin with both Pace, HR (AND Pwr which I use in hilly areas), so the watch supports it. Not sure whether this is a Garmin API bug or an Intervals.icu export bug.\n\nWatching and happy to troubleshoot. (Or even wade into the code if that’s about anywhere)\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @R2Tom\nPost date: 16 June 2026 07:25 UTC\n\nThis is not supported yet by intervals.\nI think best would be to create a Feature Request Thread.\n\n...\n\nPost date: 27 June 2026 18:44 UTC\n\nSupport mixed Pace / HR / PWR Garmin workout sync for the request / you can vote for this request !\n\n---\n\nTitle: Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect - Page 2 - Announcements - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/upload-planned-workouts-to-garmin-connect/1521?page=2\nPublished: 2020-08-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nTx. Some workouts in your library (e.g. “3015”) are missing information due to a bug fixed some time ago. So thats why 3015 didn’t upload to Garmin. I have added code to fix this for new workouts dragged from your library. For workouts already on your calendar you need to make a dummy edit (e.g. just add a space to the description) and then they should upload.\n\n@Aurelien_Decagny @Paavo_Heikkinen I have added outdoor power range and heart rate range settings to the Garmin Connect upload that convert fixed power/HR targets to ranges:\n\nOutdoor power range: Outdoor workout steps with exact power targets are converted into ranges of plus/minus this percentage of the target power.\n\nHeart rate range: Workout steps with exact heart rate targets are converted into ranges of plus/minus this percentage of the target heart rate.\n\n@Aurelien_Decagny Future fitness updates are now done asynchronously so workout edits are much faster.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu/t/ \\\"Oct\\\" \\\"2026\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" \\\"range\\\" workout' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu/t/ \\\"Oct\\\" \\\"2026\\\" \\\"HR\\\" \\\"Garmin\\\" \\\"range\\\" workout' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"Title: New compare page with power vs HR chart - Page 5 - Announcements - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/new-compare-page-with-power-vs-hr-chart/1730?page=5\nPublished: 2024-01-07T18:16:28.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nDavid, my HR vs Power chart filtered for “Virtual Rowing” is blank. Previous to a travel break in late September/early October 2023, I categorized my indoor erg rowing workouts as “Rowing” and that data successfully displayed on the graph.\n\nResuming my erg rowing in late October, data from my now categorized “Virtual Rowing” workouts do not show on the graph. Any thoughts?\n\n...\n\nYou have “Virtual Rowing” as a separate sport in /settings. Rather delete the “Virtual Rowing” sport and add “Virtual Rowing” to the activity types for “Rowing”:\n\n...\n\nThese are ‘cleaned up’ chunks of 30sec where you plot the relation between heartrate and power. If the plot moves further down right for different dat ranges, it means that you are fitter because you produce more power for a given % of LTHR (or maxHR). It’s a nice feature where you can clearly see that there is a linear relationship for power to HR when within the aerobic range. Here’s mine for Running activities over the past three seasons, showing that my running is definitely getting better this season. \n\nSame for outdoor/indoor cycling:\n\n...\n\nWhat do you use to measure your running power - Garmin watch I presume?\n\n...\n\n> These are ‘cleaned up’ chunks of 30sec\n\nTx for answering this. The chunks are actually 1 minute long and there is quite a bit of processing to get rid of “bad” ones (e.g. cadence much lower or higher than normal, any stops etc.) so only clean “you were riding/running for the whole 1 minute at normal cadence” stuff is kept. And HR lag is adjusted for.\n\n...\n\n@david currently, when selecting a season, the date range (e.g., Oct 31, 2022 - Oct 29, 2023) is shown. Would it be possible to display a badge with the season name instead (e.g., Season 2022/23), similar to how year selections work?\n\n...\n\n@david might\n\n...\n\nCan anyone explain to me how this chart works? These are all overlapping dates starting from Jan 7-Feb 4, to the most recent Jan 21 to Feb 4. How is a data point at one HR decided to be associated with a particular power value (or vice versa), rather than a more recent one? I mean if I did 180W at 125 BPM in the period Jan 21-Feb 4, then of course I also did that in the period Jan 7 to Feb 4, so how does the lower power one gain precedence? Is it the longest duration within the time period, and if so, does that really make sense? Would it make more sense to compare, for example, each power level between xW and yW. So, say I’d be able to compare my heart rate at 150-160W for 40-50 minutes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu October 2026 workout export format heart rate custom range Garmin zone October 2026' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu October 2026 workout export format heart rate custom range Garmin zone October 2026' numResults=10\"","aggregated_output":"Title: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nIntervals.icu now has a public API for other applications. To use it you need to generate an API key in /settings (look for “Developer Settings” near the bottom). Your API key allows other applications to access your Intervals.icu data. Be careful what you do with it! If you suspect that your key may have been compromised immediately clear or re-generate it.\n\nBy using the API you agree to the Intervals.icu API Terms and Conditions .\n\nPlease not that data from Strava is not available from the API. Their terms and conditions do not allow it.\n\nUpdate 9th October 2026: Garmin terms and conditions have been updated and It is likely that we will have to block access to Garmin data via the API soon.\n\nUpdate 14th October 2026: The revisions to the agreement are part of the planned reopening of the Garmin Connect Developer Program. There will be an easier path for individuals and apps to access data from Garmin devices.\n\nThe API uses basic authentication for personal use. The username is “API_KEY” and the password your API key. Example using curl:\n\n`$ curl -u API_KEY:1l0nlqjq3j1obdhg08rz5rfhx https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/2049151/activities.csv`\n\n...\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/calendars` List all calendars\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` List all calendar events. Requires parameters `oldest` and `newest` in yyyy-MM-dd format (local dates i.e. no timezone). The range may be expanded if oldest and/or newest fall inside a multiday event to include the whole event. Only events from enabled calendars are returned. Supports optional calendar_id parameter to filter for events for a particular calendar.\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Fetch an event by id.\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}/download{ext}` Download a planned workout in .zwo, .mrc or .erg format.\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` Create event and return it.\n`PUT /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Update event and return it.\n`DELETE /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{eventId}` Delete the event.\n\n...\n\nUpdate 22 April 2021:\n\n- The workout description is now parsed. This means that workouts created or updated via the API have training load calculated and get “time in zones” and so on. This also applies to workouts synced from an external calendar.\n- You can now create/update workouts from zwo, mrc and erg files.\n\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events`\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2021-04-29T00:00:00\",\n  \"type\": \"Ride\",\n  \"filename\": \"4x8m.zwo\",\n  \"file_contents\": \"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\" encoding=\\\"UTF-8\\\" standalone=\\\"yes\\\"?>\\n<workout_file>...\"\n}\n\n```\n\nResponse:\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"id\": 610062,\n  \"icu_training_load\": 116,\n  ...\n  \"name\": \"4x8m VO2 Max\",\n  \"description\": \"These are horribly nasty.\\nCategory: Horrible\\n\\n- 20m 60% 90-100rpm\\n\\nMain set 4x\\n- 8m 110%\\n- 8m 50%\\n\\n- 10m 60%\\n\",\n  ...\n  \"workout_doc\": {\n    \"steps\": [\n      {\n        \"power\": {\"units\": \"%ftp\", \"value\": 60},\n        \"cadence\": {\"end\": 100, \"start\": 90, \"units\": \"rpm\"}, \"duration\": 1200\n      }, ...\n    ],\n    \"duration\": 5640,\n    \"zoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0],\n    \"hrZoneTimes\": [1920, 1800, 0, 0, 1920, 0, 0, 0]\n  },\n  \"icu_intensity\": 86.04798\n}\n\n...\n\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders` List all folders and workouts\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders` Create a folder\n`PUT /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}` Update folder\n`DELETE /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}` Delete folder\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}/shared-with` Show who folder has been shared with\n`PUT /api/v1/athlete/{id}/folders/{folderId}/shared-with` Update folder sharing\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts` List all workouts (excluding those shared by others)\n`GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts/{workoutId}` Get a workout\n`POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts` Create a workout\n`PUT /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts/{workoutId}` Update a workout\n`DELETE /api/v1/athlete/{id}/workouts/{workoutId}` Delete a workout\n`POST /api/v1/download-workout{ext}` Download a workout in .zwo .mrc or .erg format\n\n...\n\nThere is a catch: Workouts have a “workout_doc” field which is the parsed workout steps. Currently the parser is only available client side. So before you can download a workout in a different format or have it sync to Garmin Connect you need to edit it using the Intervals.icu app so the workout_doc field is populated.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Create workout and export as .FIT file\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/create-workout-and-export-as-fit-file/33323\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nwhen putting the target_value in “Workout Step Fields” at 0, the custom target values are used\n\nsee FIT SDK documentation\n\n> File Types | FIT SDK | Garmin Developers\n> \n> FIT File Types Description\n\n#### Target Values vs Custom Target Values\n\nUnless defining repeat steps, the target_value dynamic field typically refers to setting a target zone. These target zones represent target limits that have already been established through other means, such as in a settings file, through a user interface, or predefined on fitness equipment. If zones are predefined their numbering should start at 1 (since 0 is reserved to indicate a custom zone). The workout_step can then be used to set a target heart rate, power, or other zone value. In this case, the custom_target_value_high and custom_target_value_low fields should be set to 0.\n\nIf a specific target range is desired, the custom_target_low and custom_target_high fields may be used to set the upper and lower boundaries of the desired target range, and the target_value would be set to 0. Refer to the FIT SDK for specific field/zone values. When creating swim workouts, the target is used to indicate the swim stroke for the step.\n\n...\n\nYou can’t export a workout from Garmin Connect, however you can send it to your device, the steps are:\n\n1. Send workout to device ( garmin edge ** )\n2. Connect the edge to your computer as storage device\n3. at your Garmin In the directory garmin/workouts you can find the FIT files of your workouts\n\n...\n\nIntervals.icu always includes actual values in generated files to avoid relying on zones set on the device. It has no idea what those are or if they match the ones in Intervals.icu.\n\n...\n\nWell you can if the athletes are on Intervals.icu. The same workout will be customised for each athlete using their Intervals.icu zones. Also if the workout has power/HR/pace steps the athlete can choose how to perform it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Planned workouts format on intervals.icu not recognized by Garmin connect - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/planned-workouts-format-on-intervals-icu-not-recognized-by-garmin-connect/121262\nPublished: 2026-02-02T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nHave you tried to use the “Add Step” function in the workout builder and read this guide: Workout builder - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\n\nThis syntax is useless: no target, “press lap” needs to be placed directly after the “-”. And bonus: the repeats can be added easier…\n\nFor example, the syntax based in HR zones (be sure they are configured in the settings…):\n\n```auto\n- Press lap 40m Z2 Pace intensity=warmup\n\n10x\n- 0.5mi Z2 Pace intensity=interval\n- Press lap 0.5mi Z0 Pace intensity=recovery\n\n- Press lap 20m Z2 Pace intensity=cooldown**\n\n```\n\nOn a Garmin device, “press lap” always works, it wil skip end the interval and skips to the next one.\n\n...\n\nWell you figured it out… your original text missed the “-” at the start of the lines, and the intensity targets. If you don’t like pace or any other target, at least give it some HR target, maybe Z1-Z5 if you really don’t want a prescription and want to avoid annoying beeps.\n\nGarmin does seem to ignore any volume hints when you say LAP PRESS, or maybe intervals is just not exporting them. You canceled parse the FIT file that had been exported with e.g. runalyze’s FIT file viewer. If the file does contain both the miles and the LAP PRESS then it’s Garmin’s fault, otherwise maybe give some feedback here to the devs…\n\nIf you want a minimum distance or time with your LAP PRESS laps, maybe for recovery, make separate lines for those.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end — Worklore\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nPublished: 2026-05-12T09:54:16.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nWhat we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch face:\n\n1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\n\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals UI with its \"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower bound is `start`, not `value`. That one key name switches the FIT export mode: `{value, end}` routes as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an Instant-HR corridor — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a one-word rename after days of probing.\n\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar — where `m` means minutes (`200m` = a 200-minute step!), distance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to power zones even for swims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n\n...\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, where every HR step target is `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\":, \"end\":}` — never `value`. Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from its own stored threshold.\n2. Set the event's top-level `description` in Intervals' native text grammar so steps render in the web UI, e.g. `- Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR`. Rules: durations `m`/`s`, distance `mtr`/`km`, blank lines around `Nx` repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare `Z ` outside cycling.\n3. Read the event back with `GET /events/{id}`: non-empty `workout_doc.steps` means the parser accepted the description; `steps: []` means it choked.\n4. Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\n\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR stored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two differ). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a name and total duration.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom zones for sports\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-zones-for-sports/114875\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now define very flexible custom zones, a new premium feature. These are in addition to the normal power, heart rate and pace zones.\n\n- Zones for any activity stream (not just power, HR and pace) including custom streams\n- Overlapping and non-contiguous zones\n- Zones based on any standard anchor (FTP, LTHR etc.) as well as custom activity fields and absolute values\n- Custom colours\n- User defined zone ID (not just Z1, Z2 etc.)\n- Time in zones (per activity and on the totals page)\n- Workout prescription (only for power, HR and pace streams) e.g. “- 4m CZ2”\n\nThese are configurable per sport in /settings and shareable like custom fields etc.:\n\nYou can search for zones others have created and shared or create your own:\n\nEach set of zones has a name, a stream (power, heart rate, speed, gradient etc.), optional default anchor for zones and a code. The code should not be changed once the zones are in use. Zone information on activities is stored by code.\n\nEach zone has an id (used to reference the zone when prescribing workouts and for storing time in zones), name, start and end value. The start/end values can be fractions of an anchor value (e.g. Zone “3 Tempo” above) or absolute values. The end value is exclusive. Note that for speed you need to capture absolute values in meters per second.\n\nThe id does not have to be numeric. It is important that it does not change once the zones are in use.\n\nZones can overlap. A zone with no start value covers everything less than the end value. A zone with no end value covers everything >= the start value.\n\nUse the dot dot dot menu to move zones up/down and to delete zones.\n\n...\n\nIntervals.icu will compute “time in zones” for custom zones and display this information on the activity detail pages.\n\nNew zones for power, heart rate and speed are added to the power, heart rate and pace tabs respectively. Other zones are added to each of those tabs. You can click the “Activity Charts” button at the bottom of each page to choose which zones are displayed, move them around and so on.\n\nCustom “time in zones” for an activity is calculated when the activity is analysed. The first time this is done, the start and end values for each zone and the anchor values (if any) are copied onto the activity, preserving history should they change in future.\n\nIf a zones is defined relative to an anchor value and the anchor value on the activity is changed, then the zone is updated.\n\n...\n\n### Workout prescription\n\nPrescribing workouts using custom zones only works for power, heart rate and speed (pace) zones. The “Add Step” form includes custom zones configured for the sport in its zones drop downs:\n\nThe syntax is just “CZ” and the id of the zone matching the stream type (power, heart rate or pace):\n\n```\n- 60m CZ1\n\n```\n\nIf you prescribe a workout using only custom zones then only “time in custom zones” is displayed:\n\nZones used for workout prescription should be closed (i.e. both start and end of the zone specified).\n\nIf you have several zones with the same id (possible if you enable multiple sets of custom zones) for the same stream (power, hr or speed) then the first one listed for the sport has precedence.\n\n...\n\n```json\n// http -a API_KEY:$API_TOKEN https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/ |jq .custom_items[1].content.zones\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"1\",\n    \"end\": 6.0,\n    \"name\": \"EasyPeasy\",\n    \"color\": \"#4b4d4a\",\n    \"start\": 5.0,\n    \"end_anchor\": \"\",\n    \"start_anchor\": \"threshold_pace\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2\",\n    \"end\": 5.0,\n    \"name\": \"TooEasy\",\n    \"color\": \"#2ca02c\",\n    \"start\": 5.0,\n    \"end_anchor\": \"\",\n    \"start_anchor\": \"\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"3\",\n    \"end\": 4.0,\n    \"name\": \"FakeItUntilYouMakeIt\",\n    \"color\": \"#334ccc\",\n    \"start\": 5.0,\n    \"end_anchor\": \"\",\n    \"start_anchor\": \"\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"4\",\n    \"end\": 4.0,\n    \"name\": \"WhatTheHell\",\n    \"color\": \"#dd0447\",\n    \"start\": 4.0,\n    \"end_anchor\": \"\",\n    \"start_anchor\": \"\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"5\",\n    \"end\": 3.0,\n    \"name\": \"BringThePain\",\n    \"color\": \"#6633cc\",\n    \"start\": 4.0,\n    \"end_anchor\": \"\",\n    \"start_anchor\": \"\"\n  }\n]\n\n...\n\nZone 1’s end and zone 2’s start are at\n\n...\n\nWe have added an option to round the computed (from anchor and factor) zone start and end boundaries down to an integer. This defaults to on for integer streams like heart rate, power, cadence etc.. This will be deployed on Friday AM (GMT+2) and enabled for existing custom zones using power or heart rate.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Structured running workouts from intervals to Garmin Connect - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/structured-running-workouts-from-intervals-to-garmin-connect/130705\nPublished: 2026-07-17T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThey will not be rendered on Garmin Connect in the way a Garmin Connect workout is rendered. In Connect, you will see them as formatted text, not-editable in Connect (that’s intentional by Garmin because they don’t want workouts from other sources to be editable in Connect). But that formatted text will result in a correct workout when synced to your device (if the syntax is correct).\n\nThe mix of targets (HR/Power/Pace) wasn’t working with workouts from Intervals until now, but is being addressed now as you can see in the linked posts by @R2Tom.\nOnly one target type per workout was working correctly. Let’s see if @eva can have the feature fully supported in the next days.\n\n...\n\nNot sure on how to pass it through the API, but you should not pass the distance like that. In the workout builder, it clearly shows that passing the distance before the target type, confuses the algo. Either put the distance after the target type or don’t pass it at all.\nAnd you don’t need to pass the absolute HR in between brackets either because Intervals adds that automatically deduced from the Z2 HR.\n\nTry passing this: (works in the builder)\n-5m 08:00 Pace (0.62km)\n-5m 08:00-10:00 Pace (0.56km)\n-5m Z2 HR\n-2km 07:00 Pace\n\n...\n\n@Wim you should setup your pace zones on the settings page for activity type running\n\n...\n\n+1 to R2Tom — setting a Run threshold pace was the fix. With threshold_pace unset, intervals drops the per-step pace targets on the Garmin export (falls back to HR), hence “No Target”. After setting it, the pace targets showed up on the watch (confirmed on a Forerunner). One catch: set the threshold first, then push a fresh event — existing ones need a delete + recreate to re-trigger the Garmin export. Full write-up: Pace targets lost in Garmin export for API-created running workouts — steps arrive on watch as \"No Target\" (parsed correctly in workout_doc)\n\n...\n\n> Intervals.icu Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide Use this as a short cheat sheet when writing workout lines. 1) Basic line format Most steps follow this pattern: - [duration OR distance] [target] [optional cadence] Examples: - 5m30s 60% 90rpm - 1km 70% HR - 500mtr 5:00/km Pace 2) Duration and distance Time Hours: 1h Minutes: 10m, 5m Seconds: 30s, 90s Combined: 1h2m30s, 5m30s Short form: 5', 30\", 1'30\" Distance Metric: 500mtr, 2km, 10km Imperial: 1mi, 4.5mi Important m means minutes (…\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n...\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom Heart Rate range planned workout - Training - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-heart-rate-range-planned-workout/72460\nPublished: 2024-07-23T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou need to specify the HR range in % of threshold HR. This is so workouts are portable between athletes. So if threshold HR is 170 bpm then use 130/170 = 76%. So “- 10m 74-76% HR”.\n\n...\n\nAlso possible with % of maxHR (which could give a slightly wider range of bpm).\n\n...\n\nThank you, I thought about that, just wondering whether absolute values were an option.\nThe question came because in Friel´s book “Fast after 50” there is a heart rate based workout that uses this formula:\nInterval Limits = LTHR - 30 +/- 2\n\nI may also create the workout in Garmin Connect that alows to enter BPM targets.\n\n...\n\nTry not get fixated on an abolute value, as your physiology doesn’t work on on/off switches. It can change day to day, depending on your sleep, hydration, temprature (cooling) and more. Having said that, for the aerobic threshold test/workout that he refers to, limit +/-2bpm, you can use %maxHR, like follows:\n\n-30m 70-71% HR\n\nThis will be 2-3 bpm, maybe 2-4 if the maxHR is low.\nI’m using example values only.\n\nGo ride, and see how you do.\n\nIf testing, it must be the same route, each time, so you have a comparison to the benchmark.\nBut note that power and RPE will fluctuate irrespective of how you feel, due to the wind, elevation, temperature, hydration, sleep all affecting HR.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Combining laps based on power and HR in planned sessions\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/combining-laps-based-on-power-and-hr-in-planned-sessions/117845\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nFor example, I created a FIT file within connect.garmin.com with 3 workout steps; one based on HR, one on Pace, one on Cadence. It works fine on my pretty old Garmin Fenix 6 Pro. Unfortunately the forum does not let me attach .fit files, but converting it with runalyze’s FIT file viewer turns out this:\n\n...\n\n--- intensity=2=warmup\n--- custom_target_speed_low=2778=10.001 km/h\n--- custom_target_speed_high=3030=10.908 km/h\n\n...\n\n--- target_type=0=speed\n\n...\n\n--- target_hr_zone=3=3\n--- target_type=1=heart_rate\n\n...\n\n--- duration_type=5=open\n\n...\n\n==\n= TYPE=0 NAME=workout_step NUMBER=27\n--- message_index=2=selected=0,reserved=0,mask=2\n--- intensity=3=cooldown\n--- custom_target_cadence_low=87=87 rpm\n--- custom_target_cadence_high=92=92 rpm\n--- target_cadence_zone=0=0\n--- target_type=3=cadence\n--- xxx20=0=0\n--- duration_type=5=open\n\n...\n\n> @Paleor:\n> \n> Interval.icu generated file\n\nIntervals isn’t generating any workout files. It sends a workout in script form to the Garmin API and that’s what you see in Garmin Connect. Garmin interprets that and sends it as a workout file to your device.\nWhat you’re calling the ‘Intervals generated file’ is the result of the Garmin interpreter.\nI’m not saying that there is an error at Garmin’s side, and also not saying that there’s no error at Intervals’ side, but it isn’t as simple as you state. If it was that simple, it would have been solved long ago.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Feature request: Native Heart Rate Reserve (%HRR / Karvonen) heart-rate zones - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/feature-request-native-heart-rate-reserve-hrr-karvonen-heart-rate-zones/132356\nPublished: 2026-09-09T19:42:02.000Z\nAuthor: JanHuniche\nHighlights:\nWould you consider adding Heart Rate Reserve (%HRR / Karvonen) as a native option for calculating the standard heart-rate training zones?\n\nGarmin, for example, allows HR zones to be based on:\n\n- % Max HR\n- % HRR\n- % LTHR\n\nI currently use Garmin’s 5-zone %HRR system with:\n\nMax HR: 185 bpm Resting HR: 49 bpm\n\nThis gives me:\n\nZ1: 117–130 bpm Z2: 131–143 bpm Z3: 144–157 bpm Z4: 158–170 bpm Z5: 171–185 bpm\n\nI can manually reproduce these boundaries in Intervals.icu, which works, but they are effectively entered as percentages of LTHR rather than being calculated from HRR.\n\nThe Karvonen calculation is:\n\nTarget HR = Resting HR + (% × (Max HR − Resting HR))\n\nA native option such as “HR zones based on: %HRR” would therefore be very useful, especially since Intervals.icu already stores Max HR and resting-HR data.\n\nOne advantage would be that athletes whose resting HR changes significantly as fitness improves could update their HRR zones without manually recalculating all of the boundaries.\n\nI don’t necessarily think the zones should automatically change every day with normal daily resting-HR fluctuations. Perhaps the user could choose a fixed resting HR, a longer-term resting-HR value, or manually trigger an update.\n\nA second, smaller suggestion: it would be useful if the lowest standard HR zone could have a configurable lower boundary. At present Z1 starts at 0 bpm. In my example, Garmin Z1 is 117–130 bpm. This isn’t particularly important for training, but it would make time-in-zone reporting between platforms more consistent.\n\nMy practical reason for requesting this became very obvious today. I completed a 60-minute elliptical workout targeting Garmin Z2. Garmin recorded 54:30 (90%) in Z2, with an average HR of 136 bpm. With my previous Intervals.icu LTHR-based zones, almost the entire workout was classified below Z2.\n\nI have now manually changed Intervals.icu to match my Garmin HRR zones, so there is a workaround. But native %HRR/Karvonen support would make this much easier and would keep the calculation methodology consistent between Garmin and Intervals.icu.\n\n...\n\nYou can create Custom zones with any anchor you want.\n\n> Edit: This is live now: We have started work on custom zones, a new premium feature: Zones for any activity stream (not just power, HR and pace) Overlapping and non-contiguous zones Zones based on any standard anchor (FTP, LTHR etc.) as well as custom activity fields and absolute values Custom colours User defined zone ID (not just Z1, Z2 etc.) Time in zones Workout prescription (only for power, HR and pace streams) e.g. “- 4m ZC-VM” Configurable per sport in /settings and shareable like c…\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
